# 3 readings by claude-opus-5-5, compared
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/readings: S5 is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1.2 have "offence_is_arbitrary" and "offence_is_arbitrary → evil_names" and "¬arbitrary_gov → perpetual_war" and "men_arms_make_law"; hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1.3 has "offence_is_arbitrary" and "offence_is_arbitrary → evil_names" and "□(¬arbitrary_gov → perpetual_war)" and "men_arms_make_law"; they differ when only "What offends the People is no other thing but that they are governed as the Publique Representant thinks fit, that is, by an Arbitrary government", "They give evil names to their Superiors", "There is Arbitrary government", "Civil War is perpetual" and "It is Men and Arms, not Words, and Promises, that make the Force and Power of the Laws" are true, which the first allows
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/modal-ambiguous: S5 is read hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1 and hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1.2 with no modality; hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1.3 as what is necessary or possible: its "must" does not say which it means
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/answered: S5: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1.3, to: Suppose "What offends the People is no other thing but that they are governed as the Publique Representant thinks fit, that is, by an Arbitrary government", "They give evil names to their Superiors", "There is Arbitrary government", "Civil War is perpetual" and "It is Men and Arms, not Words, and Promises, that make the Force and Power of the Laws" are true and "necessarily, (if it is not so that There is Arbitrary government, then Civil War is perpetual)" is false. Is S5, "And that which offendeth the People, is no other thing, but that they are governed, not as every one of them would himselfe, but as the Publique Representant, be it one Man, or an Assembly of men thinks fit; that is, by an Arbitrary government: for which they give evill names to their Superiors; never knowing (till perhaps a little after a Civill warre) that without such Arbitrary government, such Warre must be perpetuall; and that it is Men, and Arms, not Words, and Promises, that make the Force and Power of the Laws.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/readings: the conclusion is read 2 ways: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1 has "evil_names ∧ (¬arbitrary_gov → perpetual_war)"; hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1.2 and hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1.3 have "evil_names"; they differ when only "They give evil names to their Superiors" is true, which the second allows
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/answered: the conclusion: answered no, which keeps hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1, to: Suppose "They give evil names to their Superiors" is true and "There is Arbitrary government" and "Civil War is perpetual" are false. Is the conclusion, "And that which offendeth the People, is no other thing, but that they are governed, not as every one of them would himselfe, but as the Publique Representant, be it one Man, or an Assembly of men thinks fit; that is, by an Arbitrary government: for which they give evill names to their Superiors; never knowing (till perhaps a little after a Civill warre) that without such Arbitrary government, such Warre must be perpetuall; and that it is Men, and Arms, not Words, and Promises, that make the Force and Power of the Laws.", true then?
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/negation: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1 P3 (S3): the sentence says "no", but P3 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "Monarchy was afterwards abolished in most parts of Greece"
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/negation: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1 P4 (S3): the sentence says "no", but P4 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if Monarchy was afterwards abolished in most parts of Greece, then The name "Tyrant" came to signify, with the thing, the hatred which the Popular States bare towards it"
hobbes-leviathan-4.md:1: argue/negation: hobbes-leviathan-4.logic.r1 P5 (S3): the sentence says "no", but P5 denies nothing it speaks of: it reads "if The name "Tyrant" came to signify, with the thing, the hatred which the Popular States bare towards it, then Men conceive some great Fault to be signified in an attribute given in despite and to a great Enemy"
